      {
        "ballot_id": "B004",
        "laureates": [
          "Alessandra Buonanno",
          "Frans Pretorius",
          "Thibault Damour"
        ],
        "citation": "For effective-one-body theory and numerical relativity simulations that made reliable predictions of binary black-hole mergers possible."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B007",
    "B051"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I move B004 ahead of my opening first choice, B007. The merger problem brought together complementary advances in analytic modeling and stable numerical simulation, and the resulting predictions have been tested against observed gravitational-wave signals. This is a clearly bounded achievement with strong empirical maturity. B007 remains a profound account of quantum phase, but its ballot joins distinct discoveries with different histories and leaves a difficult attribution question around the Aharonov–Bohm effect.",
    "case_against": "B004 spans foundational theory, numerical breakthroughs, and later waveform development by a large community. The three proposed names capture major complementary advances, but the boundary of individual credit needs careful scrutiny.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "He regards B007 as a landmark because it established that quantum phases carry observable physical information, while noting that the Aharonov–Bohm effect and Berry phase have distinct histories.",
        "response": "I share the scientific judgment and ranked B007 first initially. On reconsideration, the distinct histories are a substantive difficulty for a single prize citation: the combined framing risks obscuring who did what. B004 also joins separate advances, but the effective-one-body framework and stable merger simulations directly converge on one task, and their predictive role has been tested in observations."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "She supports B004 as a major theory and computation achievement, while emphasizing the broader numerical-relativity community and later waveform developers in defining individual credit.",
        "response": "That is the strongest reservation. The prize should be confined to the effective-one-body framework and the breakthrough stable simulations, rather than the whole later modeling enterprise. Damour and Buonanno represent the former, and Pretorius the latter; this gives the three names a defensible, complementary scope."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical boundary between the foundational effective-one-body and numerical-relativity advances and later waveform development needs precise review."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My profile says I have co-authored work with Ursula Keller's group, whose contributions are relevant to B001. That connection may require recusal if B001 is considered; no comparable connection to B004 is apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
